//! Repro: `derive(Clone)` on a RECURSIVE enum with `Box(Self)` fields emits a
//! `Box.new` specialization whose type parameter V was bound to UNIT.
//!
//!   TS      -> prints "cloned"
//!   yo-self -> C compile fails:
//!                error: argument may not have 'void' type
//!                error: call to undeclared function '__yo_new___yo_t3'
//!
//! The emitted specialization (from /tmp/tn_s1.c):
//!
//!   static inline __yo_t1* yo_id_2747_V_id_1598_ret_R_gs_yo_id_2743_
//!     enum_decl_56319_…_i32_R_struct_yo_id_4932_R_struct_yo_id_4932(void value) {
//!     __yo_t3* _tmp = __yo_new___yo_t3();     // __yo_t3 = GENERIC Box(V)
//!     return _tmp;
//!   }
//!   struct __yo_t3_struct { // Box(V) : Box(V) (reference counted)
//!     __yo_ref_header_t header; void* _u42_; };
//!
//! Note the split: the RETURN type WAS substituted (`ret_…enum_decl_56319…` is
//! Box(TreeNode)), but the PARAMETER lowered to `void` — i.e. V bound to UNIT,
//! since get_type_string gives "void" for unit and "void*" for an unresolved
//! SomeT — and the body constructs the GENERIC `Box(V)`.
//!
//! ISOLATION: `derive(Clone)` is required. The same recursive enum with
//! `Box(Self)` fields, constructed and matched WITHOUT derive, compiles clean
//! (rc=0). So the loss happens while evaluating the derive rule
//! (`__derive_clone`, std/prelude.yo:6688-6784) — which is SHARED std code that
//! TS evaluates correctly, so the divergence is in yo-self's comptime
//! evaluation of it, not in std.
//!
//! This is the minimal form of tests/derive_clone_complex.test.yo's failure
//! (same two error classes; that file's TreeNode is the same shape).
//!
//! FINDINGS (two of my earlier hypotheses were DISPROVEN — recorded so they
//! are not re-tried):
//!
//!   1. NOT derive-specific. Writing the same impl BY HAND —
//!        impl(TreeNode, Clone(clone : ((self) -> match(self,
//!          .Leaf(v) => .Leaf(v.clone()),
//!          .Branch(l, r) => .Branch(l.clone(), r.clone())))))
//!      fails IDENTICALLY. So `__derive_clone` is not implicated; it just
//!      happens to generate this shape.
//!
//!   2. NOT return-type inference. I hypothesised that the untyped lambda
//!      `clone : ((self) -> …)` left the return type unknown during the
//!      recursion, collapsing it to unit. Writing an EXPLICIT signature —
//!        clone : (fn(self : Self) -> Self)(match(…))
//!      fails IDENTICALLY too. So the inferred return type is not the cause.
//!
//! WHAT IS IMPLICATED: `Self` inside the FIELD type. `Box(i32)` clones fine
//! (a struct with a `Box(i32)` field and derive(Clone) compiles and runs), and
//! only `Box(Self)` fails. So `self.*` on a `Box(Self)` field appears to yield
//! an unresolved `Self` rather than TreeNode, whereupon `.clone()` on it gives
//! unit and `box`'s `forall(V)` unifies with unit — producing the `void`
//! parameter and the generic `Box(V)`.
//!
//! For reference, the chain the failure runs through (std/prelude.yo):
//!   box :: (fn(forall(V : Type), own(value) : V) -> Box(V))(Box(V)(value));
//!   Box's Clone:  clone : ((self) -> box(self.*.clone()))
//!
//! ISOLATION MATRIX (each piece proven fine ALONE — only the recursion fails):
//!
//!   deref a Box(Self) field .......................... WORKS (rc=0)
//!     match(tree,.Branch(l,r) => match(l.*, …))  -> "deref leaf=7"
//!   forall inference on a deref'd Box(Self) .......... WORKS (rc=0)
//!     box(l.*)                                    -> "rebox leaf=7"
//!   the SAME calls inside a self-recursive clone ..... FAILS
//!
//! So `Self` DOES resolve, and `box`'s forall(V) DOES infer, on a
//! `Box(Self)` value. The failure needs the recursion
//! TreeNode.clone -> Box.clone -> box(self.*.clone()) -> TreeNode.clone.
//! With an explicit `-> Self` it still fails, so the recursive call's RESULT
//! is what degenerates — V ends up UNIT (get_type_string gives "void", not the
//! "void*" an unresolved SomeT would give).
//!
//! That "call result degenerated to unit" is the SAME signature the Round-2
//! ledger records (`Expected: <struct:…> / Given: unit`,
//! issues/yo-self-gap6-ctor-memo-reconciliation-attempt7.md), which is the
//! strongest evidence that this and blocker (i) are one bug.
//!
//! This looks like a MINIMAL INSTANCE of the handoff's Round-2' blocker (i),
//! the "pattern-era pointer-Self leak" — and unlike that one, it reproduces
//! WITHOUT applying the WIP patch, so it is a much cheaper way in.
//!
//! FOURTH DISPROVEN HYPOTHESIS: `_build_forward_ref_funcval`
//! (evaluator/calls/helper.yo:1135) empties `cap_names`/`cap_tys`, and this
//! file's own comment at :1040 says yo-self injects a generic impl's bindings
//! as TypeVal CAPTURES — so dropping them looked like it would strip `Self`
//! from the forward-referenced recursive call. PRESERVING them
//! (`cap_names/cap_tys : __fvm1.*.….clone()`) builds clean but does NOT fix
//! the repro — identical errors. Reverted.
//!
//! Four hypotheses now disproven (derive-specific / return-type inference /
//! missing Acyclic / forward-ref captures). STOP GUESSING: the next step is a
//! PROBE — instrument the mutual-recursion branch in helper.yo (~:3676, the
//! `_find_mutual_specializing_info` / `_build_forward_ref_funcval` path) to
//! print the callee fid, the bindings sig, and the RESULT TYPE handed back for
//! the recursive `TreeNode.clone` call, and find where it becomes unit.
//!
//! PROBE RESULT (this is the actual answer — supersedes the guesses above).
//!
//! Instrumenting the recursion branches in helper.yo (~:3676) printed ZERO
//! hits: neither `is_recursive_spec` nor `mutual_is_some` ever fires here, so
//! the recursion guards are NOT involved at all.
//!
//! The emitted C instead contains TWO specializations of `box` (yo_id_2747):
//!
//!   yo_id_2747_TreeNode_id_enum_decl_56319…_rtparam0_enum_decl_56319…   <- CORRECT
//!   yo_id_2747_V_id_1598_ret_…                                          <- BOGUS
//!
//! The correct one (forall V = TreeNode) IS created. The failure is a SPURIOUS
//! SECOND specialization whose forall segment is literally `V_id_1598` — V
//! bound to its OWN unresolved type parameter (id_1598 is V itself). That is
//! what lowers the parameter to `void` and constructs the generic `Box(V)`.
//!
//! Where it comes from: the GENERIC body of Box's Clone impl
//! (`clone : ((self) -> box(self.*.clone()))`) is being evaluated/emitted, not
//! just its instantiation. Inside that generic body `self.*` has Box's still-
//! unresolved V, so the nested `box(…)` call specializes against an unresolved
//! forall.
//!
//! This ties the repro directly to the round-2 work item: generic ORIGINALS
//! should not be emitted (`_is_generic_unspecialized_func`, see the "Round-2
//! outcome" entry in
//! issues/yo-self-gap6-ctor-memo-reconciliation-attempt7.md). Two candidate
//! fixes, both narrow enough to gate independently:
//!   (a) refuse to create a specialization when any forall binding resolves to
//!       the forall parameter itself (bind-to-self is never a real
//!       instantiation); or
//!   (b) stop emitting the generic original of an impl method that has a
//!       concrete instantiation.
//!
//! SECOND PROBE — the creation CONTEXT, which pins the fix location.
//! Instrumenting the single `create_specialized_function_inline` call site
//! (evaluator/calls/function.yo:1623) to print the spec fid plus
//! `ctx.currently_specializing_function`:
//!
//!   fid=yo_id_2747_TreeNode_id_… (CORRECT)  inside=<none>
//!   fid=yo_id_2747_V_id_1598_…   (BOGUS)    inside=fn_yo_id_2767
//!
//! `fn_yo_id_2767` is TreeNode.clone's own specialization. So the correct
//! `box` spec comes from main's explicit `box(...)` calls, while the bogus one
//! is created WHILE SPECIALIZING TreeNode.clone.
//!
//! Causal chain, fully measured:
//!   TreeNode.clone spec evaluates `__v_left.clone()` on a `Box(Self)` field
//!     -> calls Box.clone, but does NOT specialize it at V = TreeNode; its body
//!        is evaluated with V still UNRESOLVED
//!     -> the nested `box(self.*.clone())` therefore specializes with V bound
//!        to V itself  =>  `void` parameter + generic Box(V)
//!
//! So the defect is that `Self -> TreeNode` substitution does not reach the
//! CALLEE METHOD'S forall binding when the receiver is a `Box(Self)` FIELD.
//! Fix there — not in the recursion guards (proven uninvolved: zero hits), not
//! in derive, not in return-type inference, and not in the forward-ref
//! captures.
//!
//! FINAL ISOLATION (all with the same Box(Self) field binding, same binary):
//!
//!   __v_left.*            deref ..................... WORKS
//!   box(__v_left.*)       forall free function ...... WORKS  (rc=0, "cloned")
//!   __v_left.clone()      GENERIC-IMPL METHOD ....... FAILS
//!
//! And a fully CONCRETE receiver (`clone : (fn(self : TreeNode) -> TreeNode)`,
//! no `Self` anywhere in the signature) STILL FAILS — so it is not the
//! receiver's Self either (fifth disproven hypothesis).
//!
//! Conclusion: the field type and forall inference are both sound. What fails
//! is GENERIC-IMPL METHOD DISPATCH on that field — `Box`'s
//! `impl(Box(T), where(T <: Clone), Clone(...))` is not instantiated at
//! T = TreeNode, so its body runs generic and the nested `box(...)` binds V to
//! itself. That is the `_impl_type_captures_sig` machinery described at
//! helper.yo:1040 ("yo-self keeps the shared original func_id and injects the
//! bindings as CAPTURES") failing to inject T for this call.
//!
//! NEXT PROBE (do this first, do not guess — six hypotheses have now been
//! disproven): instrument the generic-impl method instantiation path to print
//! the receiver type and the injected TypeVal captures for the
//! `__v_left.clone()` call, and compare against the working `Box(i32)` case
//! (a struct with a `Box(i32)` field + derive(Clone) compiles and runs).
//!
//! STRUCTURAL LEAD (evaluator/values/generic_impl_registry.yo:355,
//! `find_methods_from_generic_impls`):
//!
//!     if(!(try_match_generic_impl(resolved, entry, env)), { continue; });
//!     …
//!     results.push(MethodCandidate(method_type : ftype, method_value : fvalue));
//!
//! `try_match_generic_impl` returns a BOOL. To decide the match it must compute
//! the impl substitution (T := TreeNode), and then DISCARDS it — the candidate
//! is pushed with the RAW generic field type/value, carrying no bindings. That
//! is precisely the "bindings not injected" condition the isolation above
//! points at.
//!
//! Likely fix shape: have the match return its bindings and attach them to
//! MethodCandidate (then inject them as the TypeVal captures
//! `_impl_type_captures_sig` already expects). NOTE this changes
//! MethodCandidate's shape and every caller, so it is a real change, not a
//! one-liner — gate it on its own. It does not explain by itself why
//! `Box(i32)` works and `Box(Self)` does not, so PROBE that difference first:
//! print `resolved` and the entry's forall bindings for both cases.
//!
//! ^^^ THAT STRUCTURAL LEAD WAS WRONG — it named a DEAD DUPLICATE.
//! `generic_impl_registry.yo` also defines `find_methods_from_generic_impls`
//! (the "simplified port, no specialization" one), but NOTHING imports it:
//! every consumer (env.yo's callback, property_access.yo,
//! codegen/functions/collection.yo) imports the FULL version in
//! `evaluator/values/impl.yo:880`, which DOES compute the bindings and inject
//! them via `_inject_forall_captures`. Probing the registry copy would have
//! printed zero hits. Read both definitions before trusting a grep hit.
//!
//! THE ACTUAL ANSWER (measured; supersedes every hypothesis above).
//!
//! Probing the REAL `find_methods_from_generic_impls` with both shapes in one
//! file (working `Box(i32)` + failing `Box(Self)`) printed:
//!
//!   recv=Box(i32)                        pat=Box(T) binds=i32          <- OK
//!   recv=Box(<enum:…_yo__self_shell>)    pat=Box(T) binds=<enum:…_yo>  <- BAD
//!
//! Two things are now pinned:
//!
//!   1. The generic-impl match is NOT at fault — `binds=` is the FINALIZED
//!      `TreeNode` in both cases, so the capture injected into the method
//!      value already carries the correct `T`.
//!   2. The RECEIVER is `Box(<TreeNode__self_shell>)` — the empty
//!      `${enum}__self_shell` placeholder that recursive-type declaration
//!      creates (evaluator/types/enum.yo:224) is still sitting inside the
//!      generic's `type_arguments`.
//!
//! Why the shell survives: shells are BY DESIGN resolved lazily at use sites
//! through the finals registries (`resolve_enum_shell` / `resolve_struct_shell`,
//! types/creators.yo:529/574) — but every existing call resolves only the
//! TOP-LEVEL type (function.yo:278, property_access.yo:1199). A shell nested in
//! a generic instantiation's `type_arguments` matches no top-level arm and is
//! never resolved. `Box(i32)` has no shell, which is the whole `Box(i32)` vs
//! `Box(Self)` split.
//!
//! Why that breaks the call: in `_funcval_bind_foralls`
//! (evaluator/calls/function.yo) the argument-driven synthesis binds `Box`'s
//! forall from the arg type, and a shell is a concrete-looking `EnumT`, so it
//! passes the `!is_some_type(...)` guard, binds `V := <shell>`, and sets
//! `fa_bound` — which PREEMPTS the capture fallback holding the correct
//! `V := TreeNode`. Hence a specialization over the empty placeholder.
//!
//! SEVENTH DISPROVEN HYPOTHESIS — resolving the shell at the forall-binding
//! use sites does NOT fix this. Implemented it (both the synthesis arm and the
//! receiver-`type_arguments` arm of `_funcval_bind_foralls`, via
//! `resolve_enum_shell(resolve_struct_shell(...))`), rebuilt s1, and the emitted
//! C is UNCHANGED — byte-identical error signature, same bogus spec. REVERTED.
//!
//! Why it was the wrong link: the bogus spec's forall segment is
//! `yo_id_2747_V_id_1598` — `V` bound to its OWN SomeT (id 1598), NOT to the
//! shell. So `V` is not mis-bound to the placeholder; it is NEVER BOUND AT ALL,
//! and lowers to a `void` parameter. Note the split inside that one name: the
//! `ret_…` segment IS fully substituted to TreeNode, so the RETURN type
//! resolved while the PARAMETER did not.
//!
//! The shell leak is REAL and still worth recording (a shell nested in a
//! generic's `type_arguments` is never resolved, because every existing
//! `resolve_enum_shell`/`resolve_struct_shell` call is top-level only) — it is
//! simply not what breaks this repro.
//!
//! WHAT IS NOW EXCLUDED, with evidence, on the path from the candidate to the
//! emit — do not re-check these:
//!   * the generic-impl MATCH: `binds=` is the finalized TreeNode (probe).
//!   * the CANDIDATE's value: `find_methods_from_generic_impls` returns exactly
//!     ONE candidate for `clone` on `Box(<shell>)`, so property_access.yo:845
//!     (`len() == 1`) installs `method_value` — the capture-injected one — as
//!     the property's value.
//! So the correct `T := TreeNode` capture IS produced AND IS installed, and is
//! then lost between there and the evaluation of `Box.clone`'s body.
//!
//! NEXT PROBE (the narrowest remaining gap): instrument the point where
//! `Box.clone`'s BODY is specialized/evaluated and print the captures actually
//! in scope (`cap_names` / `cap_vals`) plus the resolved type of `self.*`.
//! Expect `T` to be absent or unresolved there. Compare against `Box(i32)` in
//! the same run — scratchpad/probe_box_dual.yo holds both shapes in one file
//! precisely so a single build yields both traces.
open(import("std/fmt"));

TreeNode :: enum(Leaf(value : i32), Branch(left : Box(Self), right : Box(Self)));
derive(TreeNode, Clone);

main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  leaf_a := TreeNode.Leaf(i32(1));
  leaf_b := TreeNode.Leaf(i32(2));
  tree := TreeNode.Branch(box(leaf_a), box(leaf_b));
  t2 := tree.clone();
  println(`cloned`);
});

export(main);
